            {
              "id": "4d8eb367-819e-3ed4-87ca-74a16e23a34c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
